fake quotes that is quotations that are  attributed to an author that aren't  actually by them oftentimes by somebody  else instead are not something radically  new but they have become much more  prevalent in this internet age  people post and repost these sharing  them without ever checking sources they  incorporate them into blog pieces and  even into  books  there's a vast variety of videos out  there with fake quotes from important  philosophers ranging from plato to  aristotle to epictetus and all the way  on to the present  in this video we're going to look at 10  of the  most egregious  of these fake quotes that are attributed  falsely to aristotle  we begin with this one that i'm sure  many of you have seen before tolerance  and apathy are the last  virtues of a dying society is this by  aristotle no definitely not my aristotle  and there's good reasons why not there  are variations out there like tolerance  is the last virtue of a depraved society  which is attributed to the evangelical  preacher dr james kennedy variations on  this have been shared and re-shared  throughout you could call it the right  wing internet sphere because this is  really about promoting intolerance of  different sorts  how do we know that this hasn't been  said somewhere by aristotle well  tolerance is really something that  becomes an issue and a term in the  modern period it's not something that  you would find  in his texts so people are  reading it backwards in there they're  making him say things that aristotle  himself would not be inclined to say  society is another word that aristotle  wouldn't be using he would be thinking  in terms of a city-state or  polis or political community which is  how we typically translate that term  here's our second fake quote attributed  falsely to aristotle knowing yourself is  the beginning of all wisdom and what is  this this is a mish-mosh of the famous  delphic maxim  know yourself and claiming that it's the  beginning of wisdom that's sort of an  add-on here aristotle doesn't say this  and he wouldn't say this because he has  a differing conception of what sofia or  wisdom would be and it doesn't begin  with knowing yourself but rather has to  do with the things that are unchanging  are you an unchanging eternal element of  the universe i don't think so so this is  clearly a fake quote  the third one is also a kind of mishmash  the more you know the more you know you  don't know and this is kind of similar  to what  socrates is saying in plato's apology  you know he's claiming that at least he  knows what he doesn't know or he's wise  because he knows what he doesn't know as  opposed to other people who think that  they know but this is not aristotle  again this is something that is being  attributed to him i'm not quite sure why  somebody thought that they ought to  attribute this to aristotle but it's not  by him  our fourth one is at least rooted in  something that aristotle does say but  it's rather garbled and shows perhaps a  need to  talk a little bit more and say something  that aristotle himself  didn't say happiness is the meaning and  purpose of life the whole aim of human  existence this isn't completely wrong  from an aristotelian point of view but  aristotle doesn't talk about human  existence and he doesn't say that  happiness is the  meaning of life that again very modern  terminology that is coming in you could  say that he does think that happiness is  the purpose the telos the end of life  but he's not saying the rest of this and  you have to ask yourself why would  somebody get aristotle  mixed up like this  this fifth one similarly gets aristotle  partly right and then attributes  something that goes completely against  what aristotle thinks to him character  is made by many acts it may be lost by a  single one  in the case of this quote we do have  somebody who we can hang it on a  reverend j a hay  found in  great thoughts from master minds he was  a methodist minister apparently and why  is this not something that aristotle  would say i mean character is made by  many acts for aristotle or rather habit  is made from that and then character is  closely related to habit can it be lost  by a single action that is  very unaristotelian and to claim that  this is aristotle's position shows that  one entirely misunderstands one of the  most central tenets  of aristotelian ethics  here's another very popular one and this  one has zero connection to aristotle  there is only one way to avoid criticism  do nothing say nothing and be nothing  now with this one we know exactly where  it comes from albert hubbard's little  journeys to the homes of american  statesmen and the passage runs if you  would escape moral and physical  assassination do nothing say nothing be  nothing court obscurity for only in  oblivion does safety lie and in  hubbard's later writings he reworked the  statement so that's exactly where this  one is coming from why couldn't it be  aristotelian well aristotle doesn't talk  about trying to avoid criticism let  alone give you advice about how to do so  and he certainly  wouldn't tell you to do nothing or to  say nothing and to be nothing these  especially the be nothing  very modern way of looking at things  that's not very congruous with  aristotle's own  thought and writing  here's another one that we know comes  from somebody else you will never do  anything in this world without courage  it is the greatest quality of the mind  next to honor  this is from a book by james lane allen  called the choir invisible and it's not  that difficult to find the exact  quotation from it  why wouldn't aristotle have said  anything like this well courage could be  considered a quality but it wouldn't be  considered a quality of the mind  aristotle would have considered it to be  a habit that is established in the  feeling or passionate or desirous part  of the soul and he wouldn't say that  it's the greatest one of all the virtues  because there's plenty of others as well  that are equally great or perhaps even  greater for example justice or  magnanimity  he also wouldn't say that it's the  greatest quality of the mind next to  honor because aristotle thinks that  although honor is indeed a good it is  not the good virtue is something that is  deserving of honor and is greater than  honor so to think that this is  aristotelian one would have to be as  with many of the earlier ones completely  mistaken about aristotelian ethics  this eighth one is an occasion for some  humor it is during our darkest moments  that we must focus to see the light now  this is by aristotle it's just not by  our aristotle because it's attributed to  a 20th century aristotle namely  aristotle onassis not aristotle the  philosopher and why would aristotle the  philosopher say anything so silly and  trite and new agey as this  speaking of rather superficial and trite  sayings this ninth one i think fits that  bill educating the mind without  educating the heart is no education at  all now we can find some statements like  this in the  1800s that talk about educating the mind  without educating the heart nobody's  quite sure how this came to be  attributed to aristotle but in the last  several decades it certainly seems to  have caught hold why would aristotle  never have said anything remotely like  this aristotle is quite interested in  education but there is no mind versus  heart distinction in aristotle as a  matter of fact aristotle in his physical  understanding of psychology thought that  the heart was indeed where the mind was  located so it wouldn't make any sense  for him to talk about this aristotle  also doesn't talk about educating the  mind or the heart but rather educating a  person  our tenth is a statement the provenance  of which nobody really knows as far as i  can tell be a free thinker and don't  accept everything you hear as truth be  critical and evaluate what you believe  in this is clearly not from aristotle  and it's attributed to him by many  people who are going against the very  advice that is being given here because  they are accepting something as truth  namely that this is by aristotle and  they are failing to be critical or to  evaluate what they are wrongly believing  in aristotle wouldn't have spoken about  these matters in this way notice that we  have imperatives being used here be a  free thinker don't accept everything you  hear is truth be critical evaluate in  aristotle's text we don't often see  this sort of advice being given in fact  if there is advice it's usually one  should do this sort of thing it's not  framed like this at all being a free  thinker is a later idea aristotle does  talk about being free but not as a  thinker and of course anybody can say  don't accept everything you hear as  truth that's a truism being critical in  the sense that we're seeing here is not  how aristotle would have talked about  matters so this one is clearly false as  well  we have an 11th bonus fake quote which  we might see as not being  as fake as all of these other quotes no  great mind has ever existed without a  touch of  madness  and this one is attributed to aristotle  by another philosopher who is coming  centuries later and that is seneca we  don't have anything like this in  aristotle's existing texts there could  be the possibility that seneca has not  misremembered or made up something and  that there  was a statement like this in aristotle's  texts that we've lost but we don't  really know and to attribute it  uncritically to aristotle as so many  people are accustomed to do is to  at least in a formal sense  engage in making a fake quote  but maybe we have a little bit of  leniency with this one  as we finish up with our exercise of  debunking fake quotes about aristotle  here there's a few more general  guidelines that i'd like to impart to  you as advice or exhortations so the  first of these is to exercise healthy  suspicion just because it's out there  and attributed to somebody doesn't mean  that they actually said that and you  should be suspicious of anything that  doesn't sound quite right if you know  that something is a fake quote don't  repost it and if you don't know that  it's not a fake quote you probably  shouldn't be reposting it either or  incorporating it into your work this  leads to the third guideline demand the  sources demand it of those who are  posting quotes say where is this  actually located in aristotle's or  marcus aurelius's or plato's text where  do they actually say this demand it of  yourself as well this ties together with  the second if you actually ask yourself  can i find the passage that this is  coming from and a real passage a sourced  passage not just something that people  are themselves repeating over and over  without ever truly attributing to the  person the last thing i'll say is that i  have drawn upon a website in doing this  that i want to acknowledge called  sentence  antiqua and they have a quite a few  things about fake quotes including a  hierarchy of types of fake quotes you  should check them out so this is the  first in what are going to be hopefully  an entire series of videos going through  fake quotes on important authors  you
 

